-# my-practce-repo
\ No newline at end of file
+# automint
+
+Watches for NFT collections that have *just* gone live and mints the genuinely
+free public ones to a wallet you control.
+
+The design constraint that drives everything else: **every transaction this bot
+sends has `value: 0`.** It never pays for a mint, never signs an approval, and
+never signs calldata it didn't build itself. A route that turns out to cost
+money fails simulation and gets discarded automatically, so "is this free?" is
+answered by the chain rather than by a marketplace API.
+
+## How it works
+
+```
+discover  ──▶  filter  ──▶  screen  ──▶  execute
+(who just      (fresh?      (free? ours?  (send, confirm,
+ minted?)       new?)        affordable?)  record)
+```
+
+**1. Discovery** (`src/discovery/`) — two independent adapters:
+
+- `onchain` (default): sweeps recent blocks for `Transfer` / `TransferSingle`
+  events with `from == address(0)` — i.e. actual mints happening right now.
+  This finds collections before any indexer has them, which matters because
+  free mints are usually gone by the time OpenSea lists them.
+- `opensea`: newest collections from the OpenSea API v2, ordered by creation
+  date. Needs `OPENSEA_API_KEY`. It only supplies *candidates* — OpenSea's
+  public API doesn't expose mint stages for arbitrary contracts, so free-ness is
+  still decided on-chain.
+
+**2. Filtering** (`src/discovery/index.ts`) — drops anything denylisted, stale
+(`mint.maxAgeSeconds`), or already handled.
+
+**3. Screening** (`src/screening/`) — the interesting part, cheapest check first:
+
+- Confirm there's bytecode and it looks like an ERC-721/1155 (ERC-165, with a
+  `balanceOf` fallback for contracts with a broken `supportsInterface`).
+- Find a mint route by **probing**: build calldata for ~15 known mint
+  signatures (`mint(uint256)`, `publicMint(uint256)`, thirdweb's `claim(...)`,
+  OpenSea SeaDrop's `mintPublic`, the ERC-1155 shapes, …) and run each through
+  `eth_estimateGas` with `value: 0`. `estimateGas` executes against current
+  state, so the first route that survives is one that would really mint —
+  not paused, not sold out, not allowlist-gated against us, not charging a fee.
+  Collections using OpenSea's SeaDrop are handled first via `getPublicDrop`,
+  which reports price and live window directly.
+- Check the gas cost against a per-mint cap and a per-day, per-chain budget.
+- Confirm we actually *receive* something, via `eth_simulateV1`, by looking for
+  a `Transfer` to our own address in the simulated logs. Plenty of contracts
+  have a `mint()` that mints to the owner. If the RPC doesn't implement
+  `eth_simulateV1` this check is skipped with a log line rather than failing.
+
+**4. Execution** (`src/execute/`) — sends with a capped priority fee, a 25% gas
+buffer over the estimate, waits for the receipt, and records the outcome.
+Losing a free mint costs nothing, so it deliberately doesn't bid aggressively.
+
+## Setup
+
+```bash
+npm install
+cp .env.example .env      # then edit it
+```
+
+Put a **dedicated burner** private key in `.env` with just enough native token
+for gas. Then:
+
+```bash
+npm run wallet            # confirm address + balances
+npm run once              # one discovery pass, dry run
+npm start                 # watch continuously
+```
+
+Inspect a specific contract without waiting for it to show up:
+
+```bash
+npx tsx src/cli.ts check 8453 0xTheContractAddress
+```
+
+```json
+{
+  "standard": "erc721",
+  "name": "Mock Drop",
+  "mintable": true,
+  "route": "publicMint(uint256 quantity)",
+  "gasEstimate": "100000",
+  "rejections": []
+}
+```
+
+`check` exits 0 when mintable, 1 when not, so it composes with shell scripts.
+
+## Going live
+
+`dryRun` is `true` by default, and everything above runs fully in that mode —
+discovery, screening, simulation — logging `DRY RUN — would mint` instead of
+sending. Watch it for a while first. To go live:
+
+```bash
+AUTOMINT_DRY_RUN=false npm start
+```
+
+The env var overrides the config file, so you never have to edit config to flip
+between modes. Starting live without a key is a hard error rather than a silent
+fallback to dry run.
+
+## Configuration
+
+`automint.config.json`. Wei amounts are decimal strings.
+
+| Key | What it does |
+| --- | --- |
+| `dryRun` | Simulate everything, send nothing. Overridden by `AUTOMINT_DRY_RUN`. |
+| `pollIntervalMs` | Gap between passes. |
+| `chains[]` | `id`, `name`, `rpcUrl`, `enabled`, `openseaChain`, and the two gas ceilings. Adding a chain is a config edit, not a code change. |
+| `chains[].maxGasCostWei` | Hard ceiling on a single mint's worst-case gas cost. |
+| `chains[].dailyGasBudgetWei` | Per-UTC-day, per-chain spend cap. Charged on any inclusion, including reverts. |
+| `mint.quantity` | Tokens per mint attempt. |
+| `mint.maxAgeSeconds` | Ignore collections that went live longer ago than this. |
+| `mint.maxGasUnits` | Reject routes estimating above this; also the sent gas limit. |
+| `mint.maxPriorityFeeGwei` | Priority fee ceiling. |
+| `mint.requireTransferToSelf` | Require a simulated `Transfer` to us. Leave on. |
+| `discovery.onchain.blockLookback` | Blocks to sweep on a cold start. |
+| `denylist` / `allowlist` / `allowlistOnly` | Set `allowlistOnly` to mint only from specific contracts. |
+
+Secrets live in `.env` only: `AUTOMINT_PRIVATE_KEY`, `OPENSEA_API_KEY`,
+`AUTOMINT_DRY_RUN`, `AUTOMINT_SIMULATION_ADDRESS` (a stand-in `from` for
+simulating without a key). `AUTOMINT_LOG_LEVEL=debug` shows every rejected
+route.
+
+## Stopping it
+
+- `Ctrl-C` — the poll sleep is interruptible, so it exits promptly.
+- `touch .automint/STOP` — halts minting without killing the process. Checked
+  before every single mint, so it takes effect immediately.
+
+## State
+
+`.automint/state.json` (gitignored), written atomically via temp-file + rename.
+Tracks what was attempted, gas spent per day per chain, and the discovery
+cursor per chain. `minted`, `dry-run` and `rejected` are terminal; `failed` is
+retried on a later pass. `npm run state` summarises it.
+
+Deleting the file makes the bot re-evaluate everything — including contracts it
+already minted, so prefer not to.
+
+## What this protects you from, and what it doesn't
+
+Covered:
+
+- **Paying by accident.** `value` is pinned to `0n` in the type system and at
+  the send site. A paid mint reverts in simulation and is rejected.
+- **Blind signing.** Calldata is always constructed locally from a known
+  signature list. Nothing from an API or a contract is ever signed as-is.
+- **Approval drains.** The bot has no code path that calls `approve` or
+  `setApprovalForAll`. This is the actual mechanism behind most "free mint"
+  wallet drains, and it needs a signature this bot never produces.
+- **Gas bleed.** Per-mint cap, daily per-chain budget, priority-fee ceiling,
+  and one attempt per contract.
+- **Minting to somebody else.** The simulated-`Transfer` check.
+
+Not covered — know these going in:
+
+- **Gas on junk.** Screening proves a mint *works*, not that it's *worth*
+  anything. Most of what this finds will be worthless. Gas on a failed or
+  pointless mint is spent either way; that's what the budgets are for.
+- **Malicious tokens you now own.** You'll receive spam NFTs. Some are bait for
+  scam sites. Receiving one is harmless — interacting with it later is not.
+  Don't approve, list, or transfer anything this bot acquired without looking
+  at it first.
+- **A compromised burner is still a loss.** Keep only gas in it. If the key
+  leaks, you lose the gas and any NFTs held there, and nothing else.
+- **Public RPCs rate-limit and lie.** Under load a public endpoint may miss
+  logs, so collections get skipped. Use a real provider if you care about hit
+  rate; `eth_simulateV1` also needs a node that implements it.
+- **Sniping is competitive.** Bots with private mempool access and colocated
+  nodes will beat this on genuinely contested drops.
+
+## Development
+
+```bash
+npm run typecheck
+npm test
+```
+
+55 tests, no network access required. `tests/helpers/mockNode.ts` is a small
+JSON-RPC server, so the integration tests exercise the real viem client path —
+`eth_getCode`, `eth_call`, `eth_estimateGas`, `eth_simulateV1`, `eth_getLogs` —
+end to end against `src/`, including the full discover → screen → mint loop.
